.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt NEWFS_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm newfs_minixfs
.Nd make a MINIX file system, empty or from a directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl V Ar version
.Op Fl N
.Op Fl B Cm le | be
.Op Fl b Ar block-size
.Oo
.Fl d Ar directory
.Op Fl F Ar specfile Op Fl P Ar dbdir Op Fl x
.Op Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
.Oc
.Op Fl i Ar inodes
.Op Fl l Ar name-length
.Op Fl s Ar blocks
.Op Fl t Ar time
.Op Fl z Ar log-zone-size
.Ar image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
makes a MINIX file system of version 1, 2 or 3 in the file or device
.Ar image ;
see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
It holds an empty root directory or, with
.Fl d ,
a copy of a directory.
The first 1024 bytes of
.Ar image ,
where a boot block may be, are left alone.
The file system is marked clean.
The image is locked with
.Xr fcntl 2
while it is written, and one that another program holds is refused.
.Pp
The size comes from
.Fl s ,
which also makes the image file or cuts it to that size, or else from
the size of an existing file.
With
.Fl d
and neither, the image is made just large enough for the tree.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl V Ar version
The version of the file system: 1, 2 or 3.
It has to be given.
.It Fl N
Print the layout, and with
.Fl d
what the tree needs, and write nothing.
.It Fl B Cm le | be
The byte order: little-endian, as on the PC and the default, or
big-endian, as on the Atari ST, the Amiga and the Macintosh.
.It Fl b Ar block-size
The block size of V3, a multiple of 1024 up to 32768; 4096 by default,
the smallest that MINIX 3 mounts.
V1 and V2 always have blocks of 1024 bytes.
.It Fl d Ar directory
Copy
.Ar directory
into the file system: its files, directories, symbolic links, devices
and pipes, preserving modes, owners and times.
Entries are sorted by name for reproducible images.
Hard links stay links, blocks of zeros stay holes, and symbolic links
get the mode 0777, as MINIX and Linux give them.
The root directory takes the mode, owner and times of
.Ar directory
itself.
Sockets are left out with a warning.
Existing device files can be copied without root privileges.
.Pp
Everything is checked before anything is written: names too long for
the file system, owners, groups and device numbers too large, a link
count too large, and room, counting holes as data.
What is wrong is listed, and nothing is written.
Without
.Fl i ,
the file system gets inodes enough for the tree.
.It Fl F Ar specfile
Read the
.Xr mtree 8
specification
.Ar specfile
as the
.Fl F
option of
.Xr makefs 8
of
.Nx
does, in the hierarchical form and in the form with full paths of the
METALOG that a build writes.
An entry sets the type, mode, owner, group, time, link target and device
number of what it names, overriding the file in
.Ar directory .
A file missing from
.Ar directory
is created from its entry.
The entry must specify the type, mode, owner and group, plus the target
for a symbolic link or the device number for a device file.
Regular files are created empty.
A missing file whose entry is marked
.Cm optional
is left out.
A type that differs from that of the file in
.Ar directory
is an error.
The keywords
.Cm type , mode
(in octal),
.Cm uid , uname , gid , gname , time , link , device
and
.Cm optional
are used; checksums,
.Cm size , flags , tags
and the like are ignored.
.Cm /set ,
.Cm /unset ,
continued lines and the escapes of
.Xr vis 3
are understood; names with patterns are not.
.It Fl P Ar dbdir
Look up the user names of
.Fl F
in
.Pa master.passwd
or
.Pa passwd ,
and the group names in
.Pa group ,
of the directory
.Ar dbdir ,
rather than in those of the system.
.It Fl x
Copy only what the specification of
.Fl F
names.
.It Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
Give every file of the tree the owner
.Ar uid
and the group
.Ar gid ,
numbers both.
A V1 inode keeps its group in one byte, so a tree with groups above 255
needs this in V1.
.It Fl i Ar inodes
The number of inodes, rounded up to fill the blocks of the inode table;
by default one for about every three blocks, or more if the tree of
.Fl d
needs them.
.It Fl l Ar name-length
The length of names in V1 and V2: 14, as in MINIX and the default, or
30, the extension of Linux, which has magic numbers of its own.
V3 names are always 60 characters.
.It Fl s Ar blocks
The size in blocks.
.It Fl t Ar time
The time of the root directory, in seconds since the Epoch, for images
that must come out the same each time.
.It Fl z Ar log-zone-size
A zone of 2 to the power of
.Ar log-zone-size
blocks; 0, one block, by default.
MINIX 3 after 3.3.0 does not mount a file system with zones of more
than one block; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success, 1 if the file system cannot be made, and 2 for
a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A V1 floppy of 360K and a V3 image of 64 megabytes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ newfs_minixfs -V 1 -s 360 floppy.img
$ newfs_minixfs -V 3 -s 16384 disk.img
.Ed
.Pp
An image of a directory, just large enough, owned by root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ newfs_minixfs -V 2 -d tree -o 0:0 tree.img
.Ed
.Pp
An image of a MINIX build, with the owners, modes and devices of its
METALOG:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ newfs_minixfs -V 3 -d destdir -F destdir/METALOG \e
    -P destdir/etc -x usr.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr makefs 8 ,
.Xr mtree 8 ,
.Xr tunefs_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
